# DO Manager Git Commit Strategy

## Context
- This rule applies to all git operations in the project
- Follows a "molecular" approach to commits
- Establishes consistent conventions for commit messages and workflow

## Requirements
- Make small, focused commits with single responsibility
- Follow established commit message convention
- Group related changes logically
- Separate refactoring from feature work

## Commit Structure
- Keep commits under 300 lines of code when possible
- Group changes by component or feature
- Complete one logical change per commit
- Ensure each commit leaves the codebase in a working state

## Commit Message Convention
```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body>

<footer>
```

### Types
- `feat`: New feature
- `fix`: Bug fix
- `refactor`: Code restructuring without changing behavior
- `docs`: Documentation changes
- `style`: Formatting, styling changes (whitespace, semicolons)
- `test`: Adding/fixing tests
- `chore`: Build process, dependencies, config changes
- `perf`: Performance improvements

### Scope Examples
- `api`: API endpoints and routes
- `auth`: Authentication functionality
- `ui`: UI components
- `hooks`: Custom hooks
- `context`: Context providers
- `utils`: Utility functions
- `types`: TypeScript types and interfaces

### Subject Guidelines
- Use imperative, present tense (e.g., "add" not "added" or "adds")
- Keep under 50 characters
- Do not end with a period
- Be descriptive about what was changed

### Body Guidelines
- Optional detailed explanation of changes
- Wrap at 72 characters
- Explain "what" and "why" rather than "how"
- Include motivation for the change

### Footer Guidelines
- Reference issue numbers: "Closes #123", "Fixes #456"
- Include breaking changes with "BREAKING CHANGE:" prefix

## Git Workflow Commands

```bash
# 1. Create feature branch
git checkout -p main
git pull
git checkout -b feat/bucket-selector

# 2. Stage changes by component/scope
git add src/components/bucket-selector.tsx
git status

# 3. Commit with meaningful message
git commit -m "feat(ui): implement bucket selector component"

# 4. Add related tests separately
git add src/components/__tests__/bucket-selector.test.tsx
git commit -m "test(ui): add tests for bucket selector component"

# 5. Push feature branch
git push -u origin feat/bucket-selector

# 6. Before merging, keep branch updated
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main
```

## Pull Request Strategy
- Use PR title matching commit convention
- Summarize changes in PR description
- Reference related issues with `Fixes #123`
- Request review from relevant team members
- Keep PRs focused and of manageable size

## Examples
<example>
# Good commit messages
feat(auth): implement JWT token refresh mechanism
fix(api): handle empty response in bucket fetching
refactor(hooks): extract common logic from useProfile hook
docs(readme): update installation instructions
style(ui): align button elements consistently
test(utils): add tests for date formatting functions
chore(deps): update React to version 19
</example>

<example type="invalid">
# Bad commit messages
fixed a bug
WIP
updated stuff
Added new feature and fixed some bugs and did some refactoring
</example>

## Critical Rules
- ALWAYS use the commit message convention
- KEEP commits small and focused
- SEPARATE refactoring from feature work
- REFERENCE issue numbers when applicable
- NEVER commit directly to main branch
- REBASE feature branches before merging
- ORGANIZE commits by logical units of work
- USE descriptive branch names with type prefix